INT. FISH CANNERY - NIGHT SHIFT - NIGHT

A steel cavern of conveyors, hooks, and wet concrete. Fluorescents hum. The belts are stopped, but the smell lingers: brine, blood, tin.

NELL PARADIS (30s, hairnet, rubber apron) sweeps the floor between two conveyor lines. She works with a metronome rhythm.

At the far end, a figure stands motionless in the shadow of a retort oven. A SHAPE in a rubber coat, hood up, face hidden.

NELL
Hello? Shift's over. You can't be back here.

The shape doesn't move. Nell stops sweeping. She lifts the broom like a staff.

NELL (CONT'D)
Union rep? Inspector?

The shape takes a step. Water drips from its sleeves. Nell backs up against a belt and her hand brushes a steel hook swinging on its chain.

SHAPE
(softly)
Nell.

NELL
How do you know my name?

SHAPE
It's on your apron.

She glances down. Yes. Embroidered across the chest. She relaxes a fraction, then stiffens again.

NELL
That doesn't explain why you're standing in the dark.

The shape moves into the fluorescent light. The hood falls back. A woman's face, pale, lined, eyes fixed.

NELL (CONT'D)
(whisper)
Mama?

Nell's mother, CELESTINE PARADIS (60s, drowned decades ago), stands in the aisle, water pooling at her boots.

CELESTINE
Do you still sing while you work?

Nell shakes her head. Her hand slides along the belt.

CELESTINE (CONT'D)
(a little sadly)
You used to.

She holds out a hand. Nell takes a step. Then she freezes. Behind the figure, a second shape detaches from the shadows. A man's. Tall, dripping. Same coat.

NELL
Who is that?

CELESTINE
Your father.

Nell drops the broom. The belts begin to move on their own. The hooks swing. The lights flicker, then steady.

NELL
(shaking)
You're both dead.

CELESTINE
Yes. But it's still your shift.

The man steps forward and she sees his face. It is hers. The same eyes, the same jaw. She stumbles back.

NELL
No.

CELESTINE
Nell. We were just waiting for you to come home.

The belts accelerate. Nell turns and runs, boots slapping wet concrete. The hooks swing in her wake, clanging softly, and the whole cannery begins to hum with a lullaby neither of the dead is singing.
